   {
    "id": "ai-ai-investment-boom",
    "title": "AI investment boom or bubble?",
    "tag": "Finance / investment / market risk",
    "hook": "**Central question:** Is AI investment creating productive infrastructure for the next century, or is the market financing an economic transformation before it has established that the transformation will be profitable?",
    "md": "## Development — how did the AI investment boom develop?\n\n- **The technological breakthrough:** Generative AI became commercially mainstream following the public launch of ChatGPT on **30 November 2022**.\n    - ChatGPT reached approximately **100 million monthly active users within two months**, according to estimates published in 2023. This demonstrated that AI could become a mass-market product unusually quickly.\n    - The release triggered an investment race: technology companies feared that failing to develop AI would allow competitors to take over their search engines, cloud platforms, advertising businesses and enterprise software markets.\n- **The infrastructure race:** AI requires substantially more computing power than many traditional digital services.\n    - Training and operating large models requires specialised chips, particularly graphics processing units (GPUs), large data centres, networking equipment, cooling systems and enormous quantities of electricity.\n    - This created a new infrastructure market involving chipmakers such as Nvidia, cloud providers such as Microsoft and Amazon, and model developers such as OpenAI and Anthropic.\n- **The scale of the spending:**\n    - The four largest hyperscalers — Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta — spent approximately **$413 billion on capital expenditure in 2025**, compared with approximately **$245 billion in 2024**. That is an increase of roughly **69% in one year**.\n    - The International Energy Agency's 2026 analysis estimated that hyperscaler capital expenditure could reach approximately **$715 billion in 2026**, although estimates vary according to which companies and investment categories are included. _(Source: report-ai.org)_\n    - Separately, IDC estimated that worldwide spending on AI infrastructure would reach **$521 billion in 2026**. This is a different measure from hyperscaler capital expenditure, so the two figures should not be added together. _(Source: IDC)_\n\n## What happened — a concrete warning from the markets\n\n- **29 October 2026: Alphabet's $500 billion market-value loss.**\n    - Alphabet, Google's parent company, reported third-quarter results on 28 October.\n    - Revenue reached **$127.6 billion**, up 25% year-on-year, but missed analysts' expectations.\n    - Capital expenditure reached **$51.4 billion in the quarter**, with most of it directed towards technical infrastructure supporting AI.\n    - Its free cash flow was **negative $9.1 billion** for the quarter.\n    - Management maintained its full-year 2026 capital expenditure forecast of **$195–205 billion** and indicated that spending would increase significantly again in 2027.\n    - The following day, Alphabet shares fell approximately **12.6%**, wiping out around **$500 billion in market value** — one of the largest single-day losses in stock-market history. _(Source: SEC)_\n- **Why did investors react so dramatically?**\n    - Investors were not necessarily rejecting AI itself. They were questioning whether the expected financial returns justified the enormous upfront costs.\n    - Google faces a particular dilemma: AI-powered search may improve its product, but conversational answers can reduce the number of links and advertisements users see.\n    - In other words, Google is spending billions developing a technology that could potentially undermine the business model that finances that spending.\n    - This is an important example of *creative destruction*: innovation can threaten the profitability of established businesses, including the companies leading the innovation.\n\n## The debate — infrastructure investment or speculative bubble?\n\n### Argument 1: This is rational long-term infrastructure investment.\n\n- AI infrastructure may be comparable to the construction of railways, electricity networks or the early internet.\n- These technologies required enormous upfront investment before their full economic value became apparent.\n- Railways initially attracted speculative investment and financial failures, but the infrastructure eventually transformed transportation, trade and industrial development.\n- AI could similarly become foundational infrastructure:\n    - Automating administrative work.\n    - Improving medical diagnosis and drug discovery.\n    - Increasing productivity in manufacturing.\n    - Reducing the cost of software development.\n    - Improving financial forecasting and business decision-making.\n- There is already evidence of commercial demand:\n    - Alphabet's Google Cloud revenue grew **103% year-on-year to $30.7 billion in Q3 2026**, suggesting that businesses are paying for AI-related infrastructure and services. _(Source: SEC)_\n- **Economic theory — economies of scale:** Once a company has invested in expensive computing infrastructure, it can serve additional customers at relatively low marginal cost. This can make large upfront investment commercially rational.\n\n### Counterargument: The market may be experiencing a speculative bubble.\n\n- A bubble occurs when asset prices and investment expectations become disconnected from the underlying economic value of an asset.\n- Companies may invest because they fear being left behind, rather than because they have demonstrated a clear path to profitability.\n- There is a risk of a self-reinforcing investment cycle:\n    - Investors fund AI companies.\n    - AI companies spend money on cloud infrastructure.\n    - Cloud providers report growing AI revenue.\n    - Investors interpret this revenue as proof of demand.\n    - More investment follows, even if the underlying customers are not yet generating sufficient returns.\n- The danger is that **revenue growth does not automatically equal profitability**. Companies must also account for electricity, depreciation, chip replacement, data-centre construction, financing and operating costs.\n- If AI adoption is slower than expected, companies could be left with expensive infrastructure that is underutilised or technologically obsolete.\n\n### Argument 2: The real question is who bears the losses if investment fails.\n\n- **Shareholders:** Falling share prices reduce the value of pension investments, investment funds and individual portfolios.\n- **Lenders:** Companies that borrow heavily to finance data-centre construction could struggle to repay their debts if demand disappoints.\n- **Private credit and infrastructure investors:** Investors financing large computing facilities may be exposed to long-term contracts and assets with uncertain resale values.\n- **Local communities:** Towns promised construction jobs, tax revenue and infrastructure investment could be left with unfinished or underused data centres.\n- **Workers:** A collapse in AI investment could affect chip manufacturers, construction workers, energy providers and technology employees.\n- However, it is important not to assume that all investment is speculative: much of the infrastructure can be repurposed for conventional cloud computing and other digital services.\n\n## Exam hook\n\n- **The central question:** Is AI investment creating productive infrastructure for the next century, or is the market financing an economic transformation before it has established that the transformation will be profitable?\n- Strong comparison: the railway boom of the nineteenth century.\n    - Speculative railway investment caused financial losses, but the physical infrastructure created lasting economic value.\n    - This demonstrates that an investment bubble and a genuinely transformative technology can exist simultaneously.\n- Strong conclusion: AI may be a real technological revolution without every AI company, valuation or infrastructure project being a sound investment."
   },
   {
    "id": "ai-ai-first-rung",
    "title": "AI and the first rung of the career ladder",
    "tag": "Employment / inequality / education",
    "hook": "**Central question:** If AI can perform the work traditionally assigned to graduates, who will train the experienced professionals of tomorrow?",
    "md": "## Development — why is AI particularly relevant to entry-level employment?\n\n- Historically, graduates entered professional careers by performing relatively routine tasks before progressing to more complex responsibilities.\n- Junior employees commonly:\n    - Conduct research.\n    - Prepare presentations.\n    - Summarise documents.\n    - Analyse spreadsheets.\n    - Draft basic reports.\n    - Respond to routine customer queries.\n    - Write and debug relatively simple code.\n- These are precisely the kinds of tasks that generative AI can increasingly perform.\n- This creates a potential structural problem: AI may not simply eliminate existing jobs; it may eliminate the tasks through which inexperienced workers traditionally learn how to do those jobs.\n- For example:\n    - A junior financial analyst might previously spend hours gathering company data, building initial spreadsheets and preparing valuation summaries.\n    - AI can now automate parts of that process.\n    - A firm may therefore decide that it needs fewer junior analysts, while retaining experienced employees who can interpret the outputs and make decisions.\n\n## What happened — the Stanford/ADP employment findings\n\n> The first version of this research (August 2025, *Canaries in the Coal Mine?*) reported a **13% relative decline** in employment for 22–25-year-olds in the most AI-exposed jobs. The figures below are the updated series in your notes.\n\n- **August 2026: Research by Stanford University's Digital Economy Lab.**\n    - Researchers Erik Brynjolfsson, Bharat Chandar and Ruyu Chen examined payroll data from millions of workers, using ADP employment records.\n    - They compared employment trends across occupations with different levels of exposure to AI.\n    - Their findings, covering November 2022 to June 2026, showed:\n        - Employment among workers aged **22–25** in the most AI-exposed occupations fell approximately **11%**.\n        - Employment among workers aged 22–25 in less AI-exposed occupations increased approximately **10%**.\n        - The employment level of young workers in highly exposed occupations was approximately **19% below where it would have been if it had kept pace with less-exposed occupations**.\n    - The research found that the decline was particularly associated with jobs in which AI could *substitute for* human tasks, rather than complement them.\n    - Importantly, the researchers found that reduced hiring, rather than widespread layoffs, was driving much of the change. _(Source: Stanford Digital Economy Lab)_\n- **Why this distinction matters:**\n    - A company does not necessarily need to fire existing employees to reduce employment opportunities.\n    - It can simply stop recruiting graduates.\n    - This makes the change less visible than mass redundancies, but potentially more damaging to the long-term career prospects of young people.\n- **November 2026: Vanguard challenged the interpretation.**\n    - Vanguard economist Adam Schickling argued that AI is not necessarily the main cause of graduate unemployment.\n    - Its analysis found little evidence that AI was responsible for the broader struggles of recent graduates.\n    - It also found that firms positioned to benefit from AI adoption had hired younger workers at a greater pace than firms less exposed to AI.\n    - This is an important counterpoint: correlation between AI exposure and reduced hiring does not establish that AI caused the decline. Broader economic conditions, hiring cycles and changes in graduate demand may also matter. _(Source: Vanguard)_\n- **The most useful interpretation:** AI is not demonstrably eliminating all graduate employment. However, there is credible evidence of pressure on young workers in particular occupations, especially where their work is automatable.\n\n## The debate — does business have a responsibility to preserve entry-level jobs?\n\n### Argument 1: Businesses should not automate away the development of future talent.\n\n- Entry-level jobs perform an economic function beyond completing routine tasks: they train the next generation of experienced professionals.\n- If firms automate all junior work, they may create a future shortage of experienced workers.\n- Consider accounting:\n    - Graduate accountants historically learn through basic audits, reconciliations and financial analysis.\n    - If AI performs all these tasks, graduates may have fewer opportunities to develop the technical understanding required to identify errors or exercise professional judgement.\n- This creates a long-term human-capital problem.\n- **Human capital theory:** Education, training and experience increase workers' productivity. Businesses benefit from investing in employees because the skills developed today generate value in the future.\n- Companies may therefore have a commercial reason to maintain graduate programmes, even when short-term automation appears cheaper.\n\n### Counterargument: Businesses are not charities and should not preserve inefficient jobs.\n\n- A business has a responsibility to remain competitive and use its resources productively.\n- If an AI system performs a task more quickly, cheaply and accurately, requiring employees to perform it manually may reduce competitiveness.\n- Higher productivity could lower prices, improve services and create demand for entirely new roles.\n- Preventing automation could protect some existing jobs while making the wider economy less productive.\n- **Schumpeter's theory of creative destruction:** Innovation destroys some existing economic activities while creating new industries, jobs and opportunities.\n- Therefore, the goal should not be to preserve every existing task, but to help workers transition into more valuable work.\n\n### Argument 2: Businesses and governments should redesign graduate training rather than simply reduce hiring.\n\n- Firms could use AI to make junior employees more productive rather than remove them.\n- For example, a graduate analyst could use AI to:\n    - Collect and organise financial information.\n    - Generate an initial valuation model.\n    - Identify potential risks.\n    - Compare competitors.\n- The graduate would then focus on:\n    - Checking assumptions.\n    - Identifying inaccurate outputs.\n    - Understanding financial statements.\n    - Explaining recommendations to clients.\n- This would change the role from routine information processing to critical analysis and judgement.\n- Businesses could also introduce:\n    - AI-assisted graduate apprenticeships.\n    - Rotational training programmes.\n    - Mentoring schemes.\n    - Structured human-review responsibilities.\n    - Protected opportunities for graduates to complete foundational work.\n- Governments and universities could support retraining and work-based learning, particularly for professions experiencing rapid automation.\n\n### Argument 3: The effects of AI will be unevenly distributed.\n\n- Workers who already possess experience, professional networks and specialist knowledge may benefit most from AI.\n- Younger workers may find it harder to enter the labour market because they lack the experience required to compete for the remaining roles.\n- This could create a generational divide:\n    - Senior employees become more productive.\n    - Firms need fewer junior employees.\n    - Graduates struggle to acquire the experience necessary to become senior employees.\n- This is particularly concerning because professional employment is often a route to higher wages, career progression and social mobility.\n\n## Exam hook\n\n- **The central question:** If AI can perform the work traditionally assigned to graduates, who will train the experienced professionals of tomorrow?\n- Use the Stanford findings to establish that the issue is not merely theoretical.\n- Use Vanguard to demonstrate intellectual balance: the research is evidence of a concerning pattern, not definitive proof that AI caused it.\n- Strong conclusion: businesses should not be required to preserve every job, but they should recognise that training future employees is a long-term investment, not simply a short-term labour cost."
   },
   {
    "id": "ai-ai-firms-government",
    "title": "AI firms versus their own government",
    "tag": "Corporate ethics / national security / power",
    "hook": "**Central question:** Does a company have a responsibility to refuse a lawful government contract if it believes the technology could cause serious harm?",
    "md": "> **Disclosure:** this site was built with Claude, which is made by Anthropic, one of the parties in this dispute. The facts below come from your notes; weigh the sources yourself and present both companies fairly in an essay.\n\n## Development — why has AI created a new corporate responsibility problem?\n\n- AI companies increasingly develop technology that can be used by both civilian and military organisations.\n- This makes AI different from many conventional consumer products.\n- The same underlying technology can support:\n    - Medical research and disease detection.\n    - Cybersecurity and intelligence analysis.\n    - Military planning.\n    - Facial recognition and surveillance.\n    - Autonomous systems.\n- Consequently, AI companies face difficult decisions about who should be allowed to use their products and under what conditions.\n- The issue becomes particularly controversial when a government wants to use a technology for national security, but the company believes certain uses could violate human rights or create unacceptable risks.\n- This creates a conflict between three principles:\n    1. **Democratic authority:** Governments are elected and have responsibility for national security.\n    2. **Corporate responsibility:** Companies should not automatically provide technology for every use requested by a customer.\n    3. **Commercial survival:** Companies risk losing major contracts and government relationships if they refuse to cooperate.\n\n## What happened — the Anthropic and Pentagon dispute, March 2026\n\n- Anthropic developed Claude, a leading generative AI model.\n- The company had been involved in supplying AI capabilities to US government and defence-related users.\n- However, its leadership insisted on restrictions concerning two particularly sensitive applications:\n    - **Mass domestic surveillance:** Using AI to analyse large amounts of information about individuals, potentially enabling intrusive surveillance.\n    - **Fully autonomous weapons:** Weapons systems capable of selecting and engaging targets without meaningful human control.\n- The US Department of Defense wanted access to AI for all lawful military purposes.\n- The disagreement escalated in early March 2026:\n    - On **4 March 2026**, Anthropic received formal notification that the Department of War had designated it a **supply-chain risk**.\n    - The department argued that Anthropic's restrictions created a national-security supply risk.\n    - Anthropic's CEO, Dario Amodei, said the company believed the designation was legally unsound and would challenge it in court. _(Source: Anthropic)_\n- **OpenAI took a different approach.**\n    - OpenAI announced an agreement with the Department of War during the same period.\n    - OpenAI said its agreement included safeguards, including cloud-only deployment, its safety systems remaining in place and cleared OpenAI personnel being involved.\n    - It argued that its contract provided stronger protections than earlier arrangements and would not permit autonomous weapons use under its safeguards. _(Source: OpenAI)_\n- **Why this became significant:**\n    - Two leading AI companies took different approaches to negotiating with the same government.\n    - Anthropic prioritised maintaining its stated restrictions, even at the risk of losing government business.\n    - OpenAI argued that it could work with the government while preserving enforceable safeguards.\n    - The dispute raised a fundamental question: should a private technology company be able to determine the limits of how an elected government uses technology?\n\n## The debate — should a private company be able to restrict its government's use of its technology?\n\n### Argument 1: AI companies have a moral responsibility to refuse harmful uses.\n\n- A company cannot necessarily escape responsibility by arguing that it only supplied the technology.\n- If a firm knows that its product is being used to facilitate serious human-rights violations, it may have an ethical responsibility to intervene.\n- This is especially important because AI can operate at a scale and speed that makes harmful activity more difficult to detect.\n- For example:\n    - AI-assisted surveillance could allow authorities to analyse large quantities of personal data.\n    - Autonomous weapons could reduce meaningful human involvement in decisions involving lethal force.\n- **Stakeholder theory — R. Edward Freeman:** Businesses have responsibilities to multiple stakeholders, not only shareholders.\n    - In this case, stakeholders include citizens, military personnel, governments, employees and people potentially affected by the technology.\n    - A company's responsibility cannot be reduced to maximising revenue from government contracts.\n- Anthropic's position illustrates the possibility of a company accepting commercial costs in order to maintain ethical boundaries.\n\n### Counterargument: Companies should not override democratically elected governments.\n\n- Governments have legal responsibility for national security and are accountable, at least in principle, through democratic institutions.\n- A private company is not elected and does not have the same democratic mandate.\n- If every technology company could independently decide which lawful government activities it would support, national security could become dependent on private corporate preferences.\n- A company might also apply its ethical standards inconsistently, depending on commercial interests or political pressure.\n- There is a further practical issue: refusing a contract does not necessarily prevent the government from acquiring similar technology elsewhere.\n- It may simply transfer the contract to a less restrictive supplier.\n\n### Argument 2: The real issue is the distinction between lawful use and ethically acceptable use.\n\n- Something can be legally permitted without necessarily being ethically justified.\n- A government may claim that a surveillance programme is lawful, while critics argue that it is disproportionate or violates privacy.\n- Companies therefore need to consider more than whether a customer is legally entitled to purchase their product.\n- The **UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (2011)** provide a useful framework:\n    - Businesses should respect human rights.\n    - They should conduct due diligence to identify and address adverse impacts.\n    - They should seek to prevent or mitigate harms linked to their operations and business relationships.\n- This does not mean that technology companies should decide national security policy. It means they should assess the foreseeable consequences of their own products.\n\n### Argument 3: Ethical restrictions need to be transparent, enforceable and consistent.\n\n- Companies should not rely on vague promises that their technology will be used responsibly.\n- Strong safeguards could include:\n    - Clear contractual restrictions.\n    - Independent audits.\n    - Human oversight of high-risk applications.\n    - Transparent reporting of government requests.\n    - Procedures for investigating misuse.\n    - The ability to suspend access when contractual restrictions are breached.\n- However, safeguards are only meaningful if they can be enforced in practice.\n- This is where the OpenAI agreement and Anthropic dispute create a useful comparison: both raise questions about whether companies can maintain meaningful restrictions while supplying national-security customers.\n\n## Exam hook\n\n- **The central question:** Does a company have a responsibility to refuse a lawful government contract if it believes the technology could cause serious harm?\n- The Anthropic case is useful because it demonstrates that ethical decisions can have direct commercial consequences.\n- Avoid treating either company as automatically right:\n    - Anthropic's stance raises questions about corporate conscience and private power.\n    - OpenAI's approach raises questions about whether safeguards can genuinely work within government contracts.\n- Strong conclusion: governments should retain democratic control over national security, but companies should retain responsibility for the products they develop and the foreseeable harms they enable."
   },
   {
    "id": "ai-data-centres-in",
    "title": "Data centres in Ireland",
    "tag": "Strongest Ireland-specific Schols example",
    "hook": "**Central question:** Should a small country such as Ireland prioritise attracting global technology investment when the electricity requirements of that investment compete with domestic needs?",
    "md": "## Development — why has Ireland become a major data-centre location?\n\n- Ireland has become an important base for multinational technology companies because of:\n    - EU market access.\n    - A favourable business environment.\n    - English-speaking workforce.\n    - Established technology and pharmaceutical clusters.\n    - International connectivity.\n    - A history of attracting foreign direct investment (FDI).\n- Companies such as Google, Microsoft and Amazon Web Services have developed substantial Irish operations.\n- Data centres are physical buildings containing servers and computing equipment that store, process and distribute digital information.\n- They are essential to cloud computing, streaming, online banking and AI.\n- AI has increased their importance because training and operating large AI models requires enormous computing capacity.\n- Ireland's challenge is that attracting more data centres also means attracting a significant increase in electricity demand.\n\n## What happened — the scale of electricity consumption\n\n- **7 July 2026: The Central Statistics Office published its 2025 data-centre electricity figures.**\n- Data centres consumed **7,663 gigawatt-hours (GWh)** of electricity in 2025.\n- This represented **23% of Ireland's total metered electricity consumption**.\n- The share had risen from:\n    - **5% in 2015**\n    - **14% in 2021**\n    - **18% in 2022**\n    - **21% in 2023**\n    - **22% in 2024**\n    - **23% in 2025**\n- Data-centre electricity consumption increased by **10% between 2024 and 2025**, compared with a 2% increase among all other metered electricity customers. _(Source: Central Statistics Office)_\n- **Why this is such a powerful statistic:**\n    - Data centres used almost one-quarter of Ireland's metered electricity in 2025.\n    - Their electricity consumption was greater than that of rural households, which accounted for approximately 9% of total metered electricity use.\n    - The figure demonstrates that data centres are no longer a marginal part of the Irish economy: they are a major national infrastructure issue.\n\n## What happened — the government's regulatory response\n\n- **12 December 2025: The Commission for Regulation of Utilities (CRU) announced a new electricity-connection policy for data centres.**\n- This updated Ireland's approach to connecting new data centres to the electricity grid.\n- The policy did not simply impose a blanket ban on new data centres. Instead, it introduced conditions for future connections.\n- The most important requirement:\n    - New data-centre developments must match at least **80% of their annual electricity demand with additional renewable electricity projects in Ireland**.\n    - The renewable electricity must be additional, rather than simply relying on generation already supported by existing schemes.\n    - Developers are allowed a **six-year glide path** to develop and bring the associated renewable projects into operation.\n- The CRU also requires grid operators to consider whether proposed developments are located in constrained or unconstrained parts of the electricity network. _(Source: cru.ie)_\n- **Why does the 80% requirement matter?**\n    - It attempts to ensure that new data centres contribute to the development of renewable electricity rather than simply competing with households and existing businesses for power.\n    - However, renewable electricity generation is intermittent.\n    - A wind farm does not necessarily generate electricity at the exact time a data centre needs it.\n    - Therefore, renewable procurement does not automatically eliminate the need for grid capacity, storage or reliable backup generation.\n\n## The debate — should Ireland prioritise data centres or domestic electricity needs?\n\n### Argument 1: Data centres are economically valuable and support Ireland's FDI model.\n\n- Data centres form part of the infrastructure that allows multinational companies to operate in Ireland and serve customers across Europe.\n- Their presence can:\n    - Support foreign direct investment.\n    - Create construction and engineering jobs.\n    - Generate demand for specialist technical skills.\n    - Support cloud services and digital infrastructure.\n    - Strengthen Ireland's position in the global technology economy.\n- They can also attract complementary investment from technology firms and suppliers.\n- **Economic theory — agglomeration economies:** Businesses benefit from locating near other businesses, skilled workers, infrastructure and suppliers.\n    - Ireland's established technology sector can make it more attractive to further investment.\n- Refusing all new data-centre developments could weaken Ireland's ability to attract some forms of high-value investment.\n\n### Counterargument: Economic benefits must be measured against the resources consumed.\n\n- Data centres can require substantial electricity while directly employing relatively few people once operational.\n- This raises a question about the economic return per unit of electricity consumed.\n- If electricity supply is constrained, the opportunity cost of providing power to a data centre may be electricity that could otherwise serve:\n    - Households.\n    - Hospitals.\n    - Schools.\n    - Manufacturing.\n    - Other businesses.\n- It is not necessarily correct to assume that data centres automatically create more economic value than alternative uses of electricity.\n- Ireland should therefore assess projects according to their contribution to employment, tax revenue, innovation, energy efficiency and wider economic activity.\n\n### Argument 2: Ireland has climate obligations and cannot ignore the environmental cost.\n\n- Data centres require electricity around the clock.\n- If additional demand is met through fossil-fuel generation, it can increase emissions.\n- The International Energy Agency estimated that global data-centre electricity consumption could rise to approximately **945 TWh annually by 2030**, more than double its estimated level in 2024.\n- It identified AI as a major driver of this growth. _(Source: IEA)_\n- This creates a conflict between:\n    - Ireland's ambition to become a global technology hub.\n    - Its commitments to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions.\n    - The practical challenge of expanding renewable generation and grid infrastructure quickly enough.\n- However, data centres are not inherently incompatible with climate goals.\n    - They can enter renewable power-purchase agreements.\n    - They can invest in battery storage.\n    - They can improve cooling efficiency.\n    - They can locate in areas with available grid capacity.\n    - They can use flexible computing workloads to reduce demand during periods of electricity scarcity.\n\n### Argument 3: The solution should be conditional growth, not a blanket ban.\n\n- Ireland should permit data-centre development where it provides demonstrable economic and environmental value.\n- Potential conditions include:\n    - Requiring additional renewable energy investment.\n    - Ensuring developers pay the appropriate costs of grid connections and upgrades.\n    - Requiring transparent reporting of energy consumption and emissions.\n    - Encouraging battery storage and demand-response systems.\n    - Prioritising sites where electricity capacity is available.\n    - Assessing the number and quality of jobs created, not merely the total investment announced.\n- This is an example of **the polluter-pays principle**: businesses should bear the costs associated with the environmental pressures they create, rather than transferring those costs to households or taxpayers.\n- The CRU's 80% renewable requirement is an attempt to move towards this model, although its effectiveness will depend on whether the promised renewable projects actually materialise.\n\n## Exam hook\n\n- **The central question:** Should a small country such as Ireland prioritise attracting global technology investment when the electricity requirements of that investment compete with domestic needs?\n- The 23% statistic is particularly powerful because it makes the trade-off concrete.\n- Use the CRU's 80% renewable requirement to show that Ireland is attempting to reconcile economic growth with environmental responsibility.\n- Strong conclusion: Ireland should not reject data centres, but neither should it treat FDI as an unconditional good. New developments should demonstrate that their economic contribution justifies their demands on scarce national resources."
   }
