  "extra": "## How the four topics connect\n\nThese four cases are particularly useful because they all concern the relationship between private financial power and public accountability.\n\n| If the examiner asks about… | Best example | Main argument |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Corporate governance | SpaceX IPO | Economic ownership and voting power can become disconnected |\n| Executive power | Elon Musk's 82.4% voting control | Founders need freedom, but not unlimited authority |\n| Wealth inequality | SpaceX IPO | Market valuations can generate extraordinary private wealth |\n| Financial speculation | Prediction markets | Trading can produce information, but can also encourage harmful incentives |\n| Market efficiency | Kalshi and Polymarket | Prices can aggregate information from many participants |\n| Government regulation | Prediction-market lawsuits | Financial innovation can outpace existing regulatory categories |\n| Gambling and ethics | Sports prediction contracts | Not every uncertain outcome should become a financial product |\n| Monetary policy | GENIUS Act | Private money can challenge traditional banking |\n| Banking stability | Stablecoin deposits | Innovation may change the funding structure of banks |\n| Globalisation | Dollar stablecoins | Private digital infrastructure can increase international dependence |\n| Cultural commercialisation | World Cup ticketing | Maximising revenue can conflict with accessibility |\n| Sportswashing | FIFA Peace Prize | International sporting organisations can become political actors |\n\n## The key statistics to memorise\n\n### SpaceX and corporate governance\n\n!stat $75bn | Headline IPO amount raised\n\n!stat $2.1tn | Approximate first-day market value\n\n!stat 82.4% | Musk's projected voting power\n\n!stat 10:1 | Class B to Class A voting ratio\n\n### Prediction markets\n\n!stat $0.81bn | Monthly volume in July 2024\n\n!stat $53bn | Monthly volume in July 2026\n\n!stat 19 | US states taking public action against sports prediction markets by October 2026\n\n!stat 65× | Approximate growth in monthly volume, July 2024–July 2026\n\n### Stablecoins\n\n!stat $293bn | Stablecoin market value, May 2026\n\n!stat $6.6tn | US transactional deposit market potentially exposed to competition\n\n!stat 1:1 | Reserve backing required under the GENIUS Act (at least $1 of permitted reserves per $1 of stablecoins)\n\n!stat 2025 | Year the GENIUS Act became law\n\n### World Cup 2026\n\n!stat 48 | Participating national teams\n\n!stat 104 | Tournament matches\n\n!stat $60 | Supporter Entry Tier ticket price\n\n!stat 20m | Ticket requests in the December 2025 sales phase\n\n## Five theories and concepts that can elevate your essays\n\n| Concept | How to use it |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Principal-agent problem** | Explain why shareholders need mechanisms to hold company executives accountable |\n| **Stakeholder theory — Freeman** | Argue that companies have responsibilities beyond shareholders, including users, employees and society |\n| **Market efficiency — Fama** | Discuss whether prediction-market prices accurately incorporate available information |\n| **Financial disintermediation** | Explain how stablecoins could move payment activity away from traditional banks |\n| **Price discrimination** | Explain why FIFA charges different prices to different categories of supporters |\n\n## Five overarching arguments to take into the exam\n\n1. **Ownership does not automatically equal control.** SpaceX demonstrates that investors can own a large economic stake in a company while having little influence over its leadership.\n\n2. **A market price is not automatically a socially desirable outcome.** Prediction markets can produce useful information, but the existence of a willing buyer and seller does not make every trade ethical.\n\n3. **Innovation often challenges existing institutions.** Stablecoins offer faster payments, but they also challenge the traditional role of banks and central banks.\n\n4. **Commercial success can conflict with cultural value.** FIFA can maximise revenue through ticketing and sponsorship, but excessive commercialisation risks excluding ordinary supporters.\n\n5. **Private power requires public accountability.** The more influence a company has over finance, information, infrastructure or culture, the stronger the argument for transparency and appropriate regulation.\n\n**My revision priority:** Learn SpaceX and stablecoins particularly well. They are the strongest topics for a finance-oriented examiner because they allow you to discuss valuation, shareholder rights, incentives, banking, monetary policy and regulation. Prediction markets are your most unusual example, and the World Cup is your best route into a broader question about the commercialisation of society."
